{{#> layouts/base head-sub-title="Migrating to 10.0" page-title="Migrating to Simple Java Mail 10.0" }}

  {{#*inline "html-head-block"}}
    <meta name="Description" content="Public breaking changes and non-obvious behavior changes when upgrading to Simple Java Mail 10.0.">
  {{/inline}}

  {{#*inline "body-block"}}
  <section class="introduction">
    <p class="wide">
      If your application depends on <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> and uses only Simple Java Mail or Jakarta Mail types,
      the normal sending dependency does not change. Version 10 still includes Angus at runtime. The checks below matter when your code imports Angus
      classes, inspects converted message subtypes, uses bounce or DSN data during conversion, or handles very large protected messages.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      New OpenPGP/MIME APIs are documented on the <a href="security.html#section-sending-openpgp">security page</a>. They are not repeated here because
      adding a new feature is not a migration step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="toc">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#default-dependency">The normal dependency stays the same</a></li>
      <li><a href="#angus-api-boundary">Angus is no longer part of the compile API</a></li>
      <li><a href="#provider-neutral-mime">Converted messages no longer use Angus subtypes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#delivery-envelope">Bounce and DSN settings stay in the delivery envelope</a></li>
      <li><a href="#protection-order">Content protection is finalized before DKIM</a></li>
      <li><a href="#protected-message-storage">Large protected messages may use temporary storage</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <a href="#default-dependency" id="default-dependency" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>The normal dependency stays the same</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> None for the usual setup. Keep one <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> dependency.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The main artifact now brings <code class="inline language-text">angus-mail-provider-module</code> at runtime. That adapter brings Angus Mail and
      preserves the supported SMTP setup, including envelope sender, delivery-status notification, and detailed submission-response handling.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;simple-java-mail&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;10.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      There is no requirement to add both the main artifact and the Angus adapter yourself. See the
      <a href="modules.html#angus-mail-provider-module">module explanation</a> if you intentionally want to exclude the default provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#angus-api-boundary" id="angus-api-boundary" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Angus is no longer part of the compile API</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> Code that imports <code class="inline language-java">org.eclipse.angus.*</code> only because Angus arrived transitively
      through Simple Java Mail must declare Angus directly or stop using those implementation classes.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Angus is still present at runtime in the default setup, but it is no longer a compile dependency or a transitive JPMS requirement of
      <code class="inline language-text">org.simplejavamail</code>. This keeps provider classes out of the public MIME and cryptography APIs.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Only when your own source imports Angus classes directly. --&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.eclipse.angus&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;angus-mail&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;2.0.5&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">module your.application {
    requires org.simplejavamail;
    requires org.eclipse.angus.mail; // only for direct Angus API use
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      If the import was only used to get at envelope or response details, prefer the Simple Java Mail APIs described below instead of adding the
      implementation dependency back to your source contract.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#provider-neutral-mime" id="provider-neutral-mime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Converted messages no longer use Angus subtypes</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> Remove casts from a converted <code class="inline language-java">MimeMessage</code> to
      <code class="inline language-java">org.eclipse.angus.mail.smtp.SMTPMessage</code> or another Angus-specific message class.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter.emailToMimeMessage(...)</code> now returns provider-neutral MIME. Provider-specific submission
      settings are applied later by the transport adapter, when the message is actually sent. Code that only reads standard Jakarta Mail headers and
      content can keep using the returned <code class="inline language-java">MimeMessage</code> as before.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#delivery-envelope" id="delivery-envelope" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Bounce and DSN settings stay in the delivery envelope</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> Conversion-only code no longer finds a bounce address or DSN request encoded in an Angus message subtype. Sending through
      the default mailer keeps the same SMTP behavior.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      An SMTP envelope sender and a delivery-status notification request are transport commands, not MIME headers. Version 10 keeps them on the
      <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> until the selected adapter submits the message. Inspect
      <code class="inline language-java">email.getBounceToRecipient()</code> and
      <code class="inline language-java">email.getDeliveryStatusNotification()</code> when your application needs those values before sending.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      An alternate Jakarta Mail provider can handle ordinary sends through Jakarta Mail's regular Transport API. If the message asks for provider-specific envelope
      or DSN behavior and no matching adapter supports it, the send fails before submission instead of quietly dropping the request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#protection-order" id="protection-order" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Content protection is finalized before DKIM</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> Tests or integrations that compare exact protected MIME bytes should expect the version 10 ordering and finalization rules.
    </p>
    <ol class="indent wide">
      <li>Build the ordinary MIME entity and finalize its headers, encodings, and boundaries.</li>
      <li>Apply S/MIME or OpenPGP/MIME signing, then encryption when configured.</li>
      <li>Apply DKIM to the final outgoing representation.</li>
      <li>Give the stable message and separate delivery envelope to the transport adapter.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="wide">
      S/MIME and OpenPGP/MIME are alternative content-protection formats in 10.0. Configuring both on one email now fails validation. Within either
      family, signing happens before encryption. DKIM can be combined with either family.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#protected-message-storage" id="protected-message-storage" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Large protected messages may use temporary storage</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Impact:</strong> Hosts with a locked-down temporary directory should allow the mail process to create temporary files or choose a different
      in-memory threshold.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Finalized MIME must be repeatable so a later transport step cannot change bytes covered by S/MIME, OpenPGP/MIME, or DKIM. Version 10 keeps up to
      1 MiB in memory by default and spills larger finalized entities to a temporary file. The normal mailer closes and removes that storage after the
      send attempt.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-properties"># Byte threshold before finalized MIME uses temporary storage
org.simplejavamail.mime.finalization.memoryThresholdBytes=1048576</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Change the threshold only when your application's memory and temporary-storage policy calls for a different balance.
    </p>
  </section>

